  "strategy": "Approach and rationale: \nThe current best solution achieves C = 0.380861. We aim to find a better bound by developing a multi-resolution optimization strategy that combines gradient-based and gradient-free methods. The key innovation is replacing the local coordinate descent with a hybrid optimization method that first performs a coarse global search followed by targeted local refinement. This addresses the issue of getting stuck in shallow local optima common in the previous strategy.\n\nWe use four distinct optimization phases:\n1. Global Bayesian optimization phase\n2. Multi-start local optimization phase\n3. Stochastic gradient descent phase\n4. Coordinated descent phase\n\nThe mathematical formulation remains minimization of:\n$$C_5 = max_k \\int h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx$$\nover $x \\in [0,2]$ with $h(x) \\in [0,1]$ and $\\int_0^2 h(x)dx = 1$.\n\nWe discretize by sampling h at n_points equally spaced points over [0,2] with step dx = 2.0/n_points.\n\nThe grid progression is carefully designed:\n- Base grid: 600 (if parent solution is available)\n- Coarse grids: 400, then double to 800, 1600\n- Finest grid: 3200 (with 20 seconds budget)\n\nThis four-grid strategy accelerates convergence. The parent solution is projected to feasible region by `project_feasible` to ensure constraints are maintained.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Isoperimetric constraint: $\\sum h[i] = n/2$\n- Box constraints: $0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1$\n- Objective: $max_k \\sum_{i} h[i](1-h[i+k]) dx$\n\nThe evaluation function `compute_c5` computes the discrete maximum correlation. The objective is smooth except at boundaries, so we use gradient information where available.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   a. Use a copy of initial_h_values (600) if available\n   b. Project to feasible region: project_feasible(initial_h_values, n)\n   c. Khun's feasible descent for $\\nabla f$ computation\n   d. Set up optimization states: global, local, and stochastic phases\n\n2. Core optimization loop with early termination:\n   a. Set up Bayesian optimization with:\n      - Noise model for stochastic gradients\n      - Lower and upper bounds [0,1]\n      - Constraint projection\n   b. Store historical best solution\n   c. Time tracking with adaptive budget allocation\n   d. Dimensionality reduction using top principal components\n\n3. Phase-specific operations:\n   Phase 1 (Global Bayesian):\n      - Maximin candidate selection\n      - Expected improvement criterion\n      - Dithered grid search\n      - Restart mechanism after 10 null improvements\n\n   Phase 2 (Multi-start local):\n      - 10 random restarts from previous phase\n      - Limited-memory BFGS with second-order constraints\n      - Constraint projection every iteration\n      - Noise handling via Huber loss\n\n   Phase 3 (Stochastic Gradient):\n      - Overparameterized representation\n      - Curvature-adjusted step sizes\n      - Correlation damping\n      - Dormant coordinate detection\n\n   Phase 4 (Coordinated Descent):\n      - Global optimization coordination\n      - Lag projection procedure\n      - Shift-frequency coupling\n      - Boundary regularization\n\n4. Solution transfer between grids:\n   a. Use thin-plate spline interpolation\n   b. Preserve long-range structure\n   c. Prior-based smoothing\n   d. Relative performance recording\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\nTotal budget: ~1000 seconds\n- Grid 400: 100s + coord descent\n- Grid 800: 250s\n- Grid 1600: 500s\n- Grid 3200: 350s + final polishing\n\nFallback mechanism: Returns best solution found during Bayesian optimization phase if optimization stalls.\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Final h must satisfy:\n   - $0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1$ for all i\n   - $\\sum h[i] == n/2$\n2. Objective value recomputation\n3. Dimension consistency checks\n\nThe projected gradient and interpolation preserves feasibility when grid sizes change, and the constraint projection maintains the integral constraint throughout optimization.",
